WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.934
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.122 --> 00:00:08.080
ADAM MICHELSON: Thanks
for joining our session.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.240
I'm Adam Michelson.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.480
I am a product manager here
at Google on the IoT team.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.329
Joining me shortly
will be a couple

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:19.240
of folks from our partner,
Cognite, Geir Engdahl

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.240
and Sindre Hammerlund.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.640
So they'll be coming
up in a minute

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.400
and describing, in
detail, their expertise

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.460
around IoT and industrial.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.250
But before we dive in on
that, I wanted to just do

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.230
a quick informal survey.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.480
So for the folks in the room,
around IoT, Internet of Things,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.130
how many folks in the room have
deployed an IoT implementation,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.290
just by show of hands, or
are planning to do so say,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.350
in the next 12 months?

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.870
Wow, that's a lot of people.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.500
So that's exciting to hear.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.530
What we've seen
from analysts is,

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.010
there's basically a claim that
somewhere between 8 billion

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.480
and 14 billion IoT devices
exist out there today.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.960
And that's a lot of devices.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.130
It's also a big range,
8 to 14 billion.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.550
I think one of the reasons might
be, when you think about IoT,

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:19.098
a lot of the implementations
we see use a gateway, right?

00:01:19.098 --> 00:01:20.890
Like, If you think of
this room, and if you

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.800
want to control all
the lights via IoT,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.720
then a lot of the
implementations we see is,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.600
there'll be a central box
somewhere, some Linux machine,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.180
that all these lights are
talking to on-premises,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.030
and then that gateway
will connect to a cloud.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.650
So what is it that is the
counting as the 8 billion?

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.310
Is it the one gateway or is
it each individual light?

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.030
I don't know.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.710
I feel sorry for
the poor analyst

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.480
who's going around counting
those 8 billion devices.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.440
But it's a lot of devices,
more and more all the time.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.440
And all of those devices are
generating incredible amounts

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.190
of data.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:59.280
And dealing with the
intake of all that data

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.740
is one of the challenges
when it comes to IoT.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.560
Even though there are so
many devices that are online,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.870
there's another statistic
which isn't so great,

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.270
and I don't know what the
experience of the audience

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.740
is, that we've seen that
people claim that around 1%

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.620
of the data that's
actually collected for IoT

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.340
is actually used to
generate some sort

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.330
of a positive outcome,
business outcome.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.790
And so what's happening to
that other 99% of the data that

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.820
is not generating that outcome?

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.735
There is an opportunity
there for everyone

00:02:31.735 --> 00:02:33.610
to say, how are we going
to collect this data

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.830
and make sure that the
data we're collecting

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.510
has a maximal value.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.800
So that's one of the
purposes, where we can

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.013
see Google's mission come in.

00:02:44.013 --> 00:02:45.430
So Google's mission
is to organize

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.250
the world's information and
make it universally useful.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.990
So when it comes to,
say, Google search,

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.060
that's easy to think about.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.610
Pulling up information
from lots of websites,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.100
organizing it so
that it makes sense,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.020
providing a nice,
handy search bar

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.920
that you can type into,
and then at the end,

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.560
giving you useful results.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.460
So you tie that chain together.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:08.220
IoT has the same
opportunity, where

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.760
we're collecting all this data.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.760
It needs to be organized
in a uniform way,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.360
and then made useful.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.240
So we think about the
lights in the room

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.740
are collecting one type of
IoT data, or telemetric data.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.480
The HVAC system
might be another one.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.310
The alarm systems, another one.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.650
How is all this data
being collected,

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.600
organized to make
sense, and then

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.468
produce a useful outcome
that has business value.

00:03:31.468 --> 00:03:33.010
So some of that is
some of the topics

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.240
we want to talk about today.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.160
But before diving right into
the industrial use cases,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.970
I just want to spend a couple of
minutes and baseline us on what

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.640
are Google's IoT
offerings, so we

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.640
know what the terms are and
all have a common understanding

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.420
before we dive deep into
the industrial use cases.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:59.050
So this is an image of
Google's IoT platform.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:03.240
For those who haven't seen
it, it's called IoT Core.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.790
IoT Core is a component
that exists within Google

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.690
Cloud, Google Cloud Services.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:13.270
IoT Core has a few major
components built into it.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.149
One component is
this protocol bridge.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:19.870
That is what will enable us
to take these edge devices

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:24.610
and safely and securely send
that data into the cloud.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.960
We often will talk HTTP, or a
more popular protocol for IoT

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.300
is MQTT, which is very
efficient for IoT use cases.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.930
The other component of IoT
Core is this Device Manager,

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.150
which allows us to understand
each and every device.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.970
Both, like the example
we were talking

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.260
about before, the gateway.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.360
Like, in this room, if we
had a gateway of lights,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.540
we would want to understand the
gateway that's talking to us,

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.430
but also each device
that's connected

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:54.670
to that gateway would want to
be known to the Device Manager.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:58.128
So as data is being sent from
these devices into the cloud,

00:04:58.128 --> 00:04:59.170
we can say, oh, that's a.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.810
Light and that's an HVAC.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:02.770
And that's an alarm.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.690
Or whatever it may be.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:08.230
Another component of IoT
Core are some edge modules,

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:11.020
where we have libraries
that you can take,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:17.080
embed into your edge devices,
that have pre-built connections

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.400
to IoT Core.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.650
You don't have to use
those edge components.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:25.630
You can use the raw APIs of
the IoT Core cloud modules.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.870
But those components are there
and extensible for you just

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.450
for a quick start so
you can get your IoT

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.820
journey going a little faster.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:37.480
Now, behind IoT Core is
the rest of Google Cloud.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.940
The first module we
connect to is Pub/Sub.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.720
It's a large message bus
that's used across many Google

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.620
components to allow
services to interoperate.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.080
Once the data gets
to Pub/Sub, then it

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.930
can be sent to other
Google services.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.500
Commonly, we see services
such as data flow,

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.250
that can transform data.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:01.360
So for example, if have a data
coming in from a light and data

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.820
coming in from an HVAC, and
they're in different formats,

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.090
we have to make
them look common.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:08.800
Dataflow will help us do that.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.960
We can also call
cloud functions if we

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.960
want to run business logic
on real time data that's

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.790
coming in in a streaming way.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.410
And then many
services behind that

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.810
to store the data, we
have multiple storage

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.440
options that you can choose
based on what type of data

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.090
it is, how frequently
it's coming in,

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.090
how frequently you want to
do analytics on that data.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.050
And then there
are analysis tools

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.620
that we have, such as Data
Studio, as well as a suite

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.320
of machine learning tools.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.260
So this is not all the
Google components, clearly,

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.980
but it's ones that we typically
see on an IoT implementation.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:47.470
You heard,
potentially, yesterday

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.540
in the keynote about multi
cloud and hybrid cloud.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.330
And a lot of the
implementations we see

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.908
aren't quite as clean as every
single component up here is

00:06:55.908 --> 00:06:57.200
one of these Google components.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.910
Which we'd love to see,
but we have many partners

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.110
and there are many
players in IoT.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.170
So oftentimes, this
architecture is a suite of tools

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:08.800
that we see between Google
components, partner components,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.850
to come together
for an architecture.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:14.560
So one way to sort of think
about the various components

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.720
that are involved in
an IoT architecture

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.920
is this simple three buckets.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:24.670
On the left, we see
ingestion to gather the data.

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.130
So IoT Core is there
to get the data.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.710
Sometimes you have data that
doesn't need an IoT broker.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.742
It's already ready
to be ingested.

00:07:31.742 --> 00:07:33.700
So for that, you can use
something like Pub/Sub

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.460
just to take the data ingested.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:40.320
In the middle, we need to
clean up that data, process it.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.960
And it's the organized
step in the Google mission.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:47.560
Here we see Dataflow
functions, data storage,

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.675
various components.

00:07:48.675 --> 00:07:50.050
On the far right
is where we have

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.090
to do analysis and
understand this data.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.870
And if we don't see Google
components in all of these,

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:56.890
that's fine.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.230
What's more important is,
for the folks in the room

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.420
implementing this,
that they have

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.280
a way of laying out
the components you're

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.350
going to have in your IoT
and know what functions

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.250
they're going to operate on.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:12.460
So before, when we were
seeing 99% of the data in IoT

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.290
is not reaching
its full potential.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:18.130
A lot of the reason why
is because the process

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.790
and clean step isn't cleaning
up the data as much as we need,

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:24.010
so the data is looking
like it's in data silo

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:26.230
from the lights in the
HVAC and et cetera.

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:29.260
And the other reason is because
there's so much of this data.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.120
It's not hard for
a single device

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.669
to generate gigabytes
of data in a single year

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:37.630
if that device is talking
multiple times per minute

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.520
or multiple times per second.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.890
And then multiply that
times all the devices,

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.110
and we're talking about
it enormous data load

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:45.610
to be able to
understand that data.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.650
So we just don't have
the human capital

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.280
to go through all that data.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.030
And that's where tools like--

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:54.080
let me go back one--

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.270
the machine learning
tools come in that

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:59.960
will help us understand,
over here on the right,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.350
to review all that data so
that-- let the machine look

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:06.620
at the data and figure out how
to find the insights rather

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.550
than the people.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:11.510
And that's why ML and
IoT go together so well.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:14.480
So on the next slide, I wanted
to talk about machine learning,

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:17.940
where machine learning also has
lots of tools you could use.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.820
So what's a
framework that we can

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.580
think about in terms of which
mL components we want to use?

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.930
And this is another
block of three.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.140
So here on the left, we
have Google components that

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.960
are pre-built, ready to use.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.470
Our data, our model.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.790
Meaning, for example, vision.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:39.290
We have a Google vision
machine learning component

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:40.550
that you can upload an image.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.670
A pizza, here's a pizza.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.290
And Google will say,
I think that's pizza.

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:47.360
And then you can go
ahead and use that data.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.250
Or it's a building,
or it's a dog.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:50.095
That sort of thing.

00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:51.470
And these components
on the left,

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.540
there are more and more
of them all the time.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.580
Very simple to
use, a great place

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.980
to start your ML journey
with all this data.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:02.060
But oftentimes you'll say,
OK, I know that's a pizza,

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.100
but I run a pizza
company and I wanted

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.210
to know that it's
one of my pizzas,

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.700
because I have all these
different pizzas that I make.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.235
So how can I train that model?

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:12.860
And that's where the
middle components,

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.420
the retrained models,
AutoML will come in.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.080
So like, AutomL Vision
is a tool you can use.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.770
And the models are pre-built,
but the training is not.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:26.390
And here, you'll upload an
image and say, here's pizza,

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:27.770
and it's my supreme pizza.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.240
And here's another one, and
it's my, whatever, deep dish.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:31.370
And these are the others.

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:33.360
So that Google will
learn those images,

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.600
so when it sees images
of those in the future,

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.790
maybe as the pizzas are
coming out of the oven

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.350
and you want to make
sure it's matching what

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.960
the customer ordered, somebody
could take a picture of it,

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.980
match the point of
sale, and say, yep,

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:45.980
that's the supreme pizza,
just like the customer

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.450
ordered with extra mushrooms.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:50.120
And that's where tools
like AutoML can come in.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.097
Any of that training
you do is your data.

00:10:53.097 --> 00:10:54.680
None of it would
filter into the left.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.210
None of it filters into Google.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:57.740
It's all your data.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.900
And then sometimes,
customers need even more.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.060
They need not only
custom trained models,

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.100
but they need to build
their own models.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.280
And that's where the
data scientists defined

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:10.040
the rules you need,
defined the data sets.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.320
You can create great
tools like TensorFlow,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.930
either the open source or
the hosted version on Google,

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:19.820
and have models that are
custom to your business.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:21.740
And customers will
often start on the left,

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.710
use the out of the box ones,
and work their way to the right

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:25.382
as they need to.

00:11:25.382 --> 00:11:27.590
Because the more you go to
the right, the more coding

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:28.340
you have to do.

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:29.900
And the more coding
you have to do,

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.070
the less you're worrying
about your core business.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:35.670
And so that's why we
offer this suite of tools.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:41.190
So with that, we have a baseline
of IoT core and the components.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.490
So now what I want to
do is invite Geir up,

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:47.030
and he's going to
describe to how Cognite

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:51.380
has taken these tools, assembled
them in an industrial IoT use

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.020
case, and brought them
into the real world.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:57.220
And he'll share with us some
of the lessons they've learned

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.590
and some of the tips for all
of us to take advantage of.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:01.100
Geir?

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:01.570
Thank you.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:02.820
GEIR ENGDAHL: Thank you, Adam.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:07.350
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.350
Hello, everyone.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.660
My name is Geir.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.510
I'm CTO and
co-founder of Cognite,

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.700
which is a software
company that works

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.390
with asset heavy industries.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.190
What that means is,
basically, industry

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.730
that has big machines
that cost lots of money.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.220
So we've worked mainly
with oil and gas,

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:29.670
also in power and
utilities, and shipping.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.380
Now, this talk will
focus on shipping

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.460
because it is the most
challenging edge environment

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.740
that we've encountered.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:40.530
But before we dive into
that, I want to make sure

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.450
that you're all awake.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.630
So, quick show of hands,
how many of you have

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:49.820
had a cup of coffee
this morning?

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.830
Yes, that's what I thought.

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:55.190
As we all know, coffee
is one of the primary

00:12:55.190 --> 00:13:00.500
or most important inputs to
writing a good software code.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:06.050
But what are the
inputs to coffee?

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:11.510
It turns out that coffee sits
at the top of a very long,

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.600
complex industrial value chain.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.930
And you don't have to backtrack
very far to understand this,

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:20.380
because the beans, before
they came to Moscone,

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.730
they were on a truck.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.760
And that truck needed fuel,
hence the oil and gas industry.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.830
Or, where I'm from,
in Norway, I believe

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.520
60% of new vehicles sold
last month were electric.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.400
So maybe you have power
and utilities too.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:39.710
And the truck needed to be
manufactured from steel,

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.320
so you have mining, steel mills.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:45.050
And before the beans
got onto the truck,

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:47.690
they were probably on a ship.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:53.010
So there's this
huge value chain,

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.340
which touches all the
industries that we are in.

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:56.500
And it's not just coffee.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.250
It's everything that
we surround ourselves

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:02.180
with that kind of make our lives
comfortable and convenient.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.760
And that's why I think
it's such a privilege

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:10.100
to use data and algorithms
to drive industry

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:14.000
to be more efficient, produce
more for less energy input,

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.640
make it safer.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:21.650
It's really quite
the potential there.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:24.340
So when we started
Cognite, we set out,

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.380
and we had this
kind of naive belief

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:31.030
that we would use sensor
data and would do ML on that,

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:32.660
and things would be great.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.740
Now, it turns out, industry
is a little bit different

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:40.120
from consumer in that there
are so many different data

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.170
silos, different protocols,
different systems that you

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:45.388
have to integrate with.

00:14:45.388 --> 00:14:47.180
And in order to understand
the sensor data,

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:50.045
you need a lot of
context around it.

00:14:50.045 --> 00:14:51.670
So maybe you need
the engineering data.

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.470
Maybe you need the CAD
model to figure out

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:55.990
where the sensor
actually is located,

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.810
or need the topology
of how stuff

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.400
flows through a power plant.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.700
And so we were very
lucky to start out

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:09.070
with a large industrial
customer very

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:13.786
early to be exposed to
this reality of industry.

00:15:13.786 --> 00:15:20.010
And I think the core problem
is that the lifecycle

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.980
of industrial equipment
is so much longer

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.970
than the lifecycle of software.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:30.210
A plant that's five
years old is new.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.610
Software that's five
years old is old.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.730
So then, with this data
complexity that we witness,

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:44.620
our mission quickly became
to liberate all this data

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:49.090
from the industrial silos
and move it to somewhere

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.220
where it's always available.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:55.630
And clean it so that it becomes
understandable, so that it's

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.120
easy to build value from it.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:03.410
As you can see, it's quite
a complex reality out there.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.770
As Adam was saying, the
scale of data is enormous.

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:10.190
A single sensor sending
one value per second

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:14.000
will produce one gigabyte
of data every two years.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.910
And that doesn't
sound like a lot,

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:20.930
but the typical ship
has 5,000 of these.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:24.010
An oil platform
can have 100,000.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:26.140
So it becomes a lot of
data, and that actually

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.720
informed our choice of
Google Cloud as the vendor,

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.750
because we believe in
the ability of Google

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:36.190
to run distributed systems at
a scale that's one step ahead

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:37.880
of everyone else.

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:40.330
So we want to reuse the
same infrastructure that's

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:44.770
powering YouTube, Google
search, and Gmail to store

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:46.000
all this data.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.360
For instance, we're storing all
the IoT data, all the sensor

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:52.150
data time series,
in a system called

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:56.290
Bigtable, which is a
ridiculously distributed

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:57.760
key value store.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:02.380
And how we structure
data into Bigtable

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:04.450
to be able to query
it efficiently

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:08.680
and store data efficiently,
could fill an entire talk.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:10.990
And in fact, it does.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:13.172
I did a talk on that
last year at Next.

00:17:13.172 --> 00:17:15.130
So if you're interested
in the details of that,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.740
you can Google
Cognite Next Bigtable

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.000
and you'll find that talk.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:28.560
As someone who loves
technology, it's really easy

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:33.000
to get kind of sidetracked
into building technology

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.720
for technology's sake.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.960
And that usually doesn't lead
to great outcomes in the end.

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:45.240
And I've been guilty
of that in my past.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:49.170
So it makes sense to sit down
with the people in industry,

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:52.350
figure out what the real
valuable use cases are.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:54.630
And some of these
use cases will span--

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:57.420
we see in every vertical,
every industry that we are in,

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:01.290
such a fuel efficiency, or
energy efficiency in general.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.280
Nobody wants to waste energy.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:04.830
Energy is a cost.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:08.360
In fact, in shipping, it
is the number one cost.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:12.720
A single percent
reduction in fuel costs

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:20.520
for an average freight ship is
a $50k per ship annual savings.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.350
And for a fleet of 100
ships, that's $5 million.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:26.370
And by the way, we're
not targeting 1%.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:28.680
So our customers
estimate anywhere

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:34.200
from 5% to 15% savings,
depending on the type of ship

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.412
and the age of the ship.

00:18:36.412 --> 00:18:37.870
And then there are
other use cases,

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:39.990
which are specific
to each industry,

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.330
such as weather impact on cargo.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:44.740
You want to be able to
tell your customer that you

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:47.150
didn't damage the goods.

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:50.370
And you can measure the motion
of the ship at any time.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.510
So you can kind
of say, hey, look.

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.210
I didn't go through
too rough seas.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:05.400
This is what a data
silo looks like.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:09.960
We had this great piece
of equipment, a sensor,

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:14.820
that is using lasers to measure
the torque on the propeller

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.990
axle on the ship.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:20.300
So it's like the
laser underwater

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.310
sending a beam along the axle.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:23.150
It's reflected.

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:24.680
It's detected.

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.860
It measures the
microscopic deformation

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:33.580
of the axle due to the
force enacted on it.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:37.750
Now, contrast that with
the way that this data

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.600
is sent to the cloud.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:44.830
Because what happens
is, every day, this guy

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:48.190
walks down to the
engine control room

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.547
and writes down the value
on a piece of paper,

00:19:50.547 --> 00:19:51.630
and it goes into a report.

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:02.530
But that's not-- the reason
this particular measure is

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:07.090
important is, if you want to
solve the energy efficiency use

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:12.160
case for a ship, you need
to be able to break down

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:16.540
the fuel used to ship motion
into its two main parts.

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.310
That's the engine efficiency.

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:22.540
So how much force do
you get onto the water

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:25.150
via the propeller
for each unit of fuel

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:28.550
used, and the hull performance.

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:32.740
So for the force that the
propeller acts on the water,

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:35.420
how far do you go?

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:39.440
And a once per day resolution
is nowhere near what

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:43.940
you need to solve the use case.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:46.580
Because the engine
settings change too often.

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:48.230
Weather changes all the time.

00:20:50.930 --> 00:20:56.050
So here, to get the data out,
we had to install an adapter.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:59.510
And this is a pretty
common pattern.

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:03.010
So the data set's in
a separate network.

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.590
You can't talk to it on
the ship's network, which

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:09.340
converts the data and sends it
onto the ship ethernet, where

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:13.510
it can be picked
up by an edge box.

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:15.770
And you have many
of these systems

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:19.080
that you need to
liberate data from.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.150
Here's another issue.

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:22.010
Connectivity.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:24.710
The good news is that you can
be connected virtually anywhere

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:27.080
on the planet.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:31.730
The bad news, it's kind of
expensive and the bandwidth

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:34.790
is not great.

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:36.920
One of the things that
surprised me about this

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:41.480
is that you actually get
quite decent latency.

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:44.420
Your data can go from
the laser to the cloud

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.650
in 200 milliseconds.

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.870
I find that pretty awesome.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:54.730
But we have too much data to
send over this connection.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:57.620
And most of the data is not
that useful for this use case

00:21:57.620 --> 00:21:58.120
anyway.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:09.440
So given those
constraints, here's

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.250
how we approached
solving the situation.

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:17.450
First, given the
bandwidth constraints,

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:19.750
there is a need for
an edge component.

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:26.980
It needs to manage
the bandwidth.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.820
So some of the data
is high priority

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:31.740
and should be sent
directly to the cloud.

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:34.620
Some of the data
needs to be buffered

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:37.800
on the edge, stored there
until connectivity is better.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.810
When the ship is
close to shore, it

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:41.610
will get cellular
coverage, which

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.400
is much cheaper
and much wider when

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:48.600
you can bulk upload that data.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:50.780
You also may want to do
some processing on the data.

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:55.920
Down sampling or compressing
the data in other ways.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:58.760
For instance, vibration
sensors on rotating equipment

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.750
will often send you
data at 50 kilohertz.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.070
So that's 50,000 data
points per second.

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:05.915
That's like, 10 times
more than the rest

00:23:05.915 --> 00:23:07.040
of the sensors on the ship.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:08.750
You can't send all of that data.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:10.280
And you don't need to.

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:13.220
Because if you do a Fourier
transform, convert the signal

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:15.050
into the frequency
space, and send

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:20.330
the coefficients of each
frequency that constitutes

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:26.540
the signal, then you get
almost exactly the same curve

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:31.100
with 1/1000 of the
data transmitted,

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:32.890
which is also a very cool stat.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:38.680
And you may also want
to use it as a cache.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.600
If you have applications
on the ship,

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:43.960
if you have like, a digital
worker application running

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:46.750
on Android phones where
the workers on the ship

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:50.590
can get all the sensor data
from the ship at any time,

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:53.600
they may not want to go
via the cloud for that.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:57.160
So you can ask the
edge box directly.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.070
And there are also more
interesting use cases

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:02.320
which we will get into, which
does more interesting computes

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:04.390
and predictions on the edge.

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:11.830
Also, this is not your
typical IoT consumer scenario

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:14.920
where you have to have
very, very light hardware.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:18.670
It actually makes sense to
invest in rugged hardware that

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:21.370
won't break, in this case.

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:23.590
And it also needs
a bit of storage

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:25.480
because it needs to
buffer all this data.

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:29.950
An average ship will
produce something

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:33.940
like 500 megabytes
per day of data.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:37.040
And it can be at sea for
up to two months at a time.

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.490
So you need some
storage there and.

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:42.100
You also don't want to lose
that data if one disk dies.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:45.830
So it's a RAID configuration.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:48.230
It has a rugged PC
with no moving parts.

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:55.320
So here's what the
architecture looks like.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:59.953
You have all these silos at
the bottom, the green boxes.

00:24:59.953 --> 00:25:01.620
They're typically on
their own networks.

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:03.970
You can't access them
via the ship network.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:07.025
So you need a physical adapter.

00:25:07.025 --> 00:25:08.400
And in our case,
those are always

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:14.380
set up to be read only, which
makes the whole situation less

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:17.080
scary from a security
point of view.

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:18.840
Then it's picked
up by the edge box.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.370
And what it needs
to do is translate

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:24.910
from all of these different
industrial protocols.

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:29.500
Here we have the modbus,
the OPC, OPC-UA, MQTT,

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:34.810
and a wide variety of other
dialects that you need to talk.

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:39.850
Once you've translated the
data into a sane format,

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:44.740
put it on another queue,
and then it's processed.

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:47.650
It's matched against the list
of high priority [? tax. ?]

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:51.250
And some is sent directly
over the satellite link, which

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:55.360
is managed by Cloud IoT Core.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:59.170
And the other data
is stored and sent

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.910
over a cell phone connection.

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:03.410
One of the things
that we didn't do,

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:08.400
which we are considering
doing, is to move to EdgeX.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:12.670
EdgeX is a framework
for edge solutions,

00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:15.700
which will do some of
the translation for you.

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.670
And it has a lot of components
that you can put together

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:22.570
to solve for many
of these scenarios.

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:23.460
One of the things--

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:27.800
so Cognite is almost
200 people, and it's

00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:30.110
just over two years old.

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:33.210
One of the things that we
value very highly is speed.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.750
And one of the things
that we do to get speed

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:38.690
is to always try to use
managed solutions where we can.

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:42.280
You don't want to
reinvent the wheel.

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:44.930
And when you're looking
at this next slide,

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.590
you might wonder if
we do anything at all.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:53.290
Because it looks like Google
is doing all the work.

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:54.540
But there is some stuff there.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:56.190
Like, Cloud
Functions, we actually

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.230
write the stuff that's
in the Cloud Function,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:02.330
and the Kubernetes engine, where
we have our business logic.

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:08.690
So on the cloud side,
basically, what IoT Core

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:11.170
gives us is a
managed connection.

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:12.830
We don't need to
scale that thing.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:16.520
And it has encryption.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:18.530
Authentication is taken care of.

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:20.600
And it conveniently
puts the data

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.850
on the Pub/Sub queue, which we
use for the rest of our system.

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:25.630
So it's, to us, it's--

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:27.860
we just interact with
the Pub/Sub queue.

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:30.860
Which triggers Cloud
Function, which

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:36.140
translates the data on the
queue into API culls to our API

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:38.060
gateway.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:47.630
So our solution is
used by many customers

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:50.690
in many verticals,
many industries,

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:55.460
which have slightly different
ways of sending data.

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.070
So not all of it is
going through IoT Core.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.770
And that's why the
API kind of accessed

00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:05.390
the one gate, which is the
common denominator there.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:07.610
That's why you need
that translation.

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:11.690
Same thing with the daily
logs, the backfilled data.

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:14.540
It's uploaded to GCP--

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:16.940
Google Cloud Storage, I mean.

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:19.490
And that triggers a
Cloud Function whenever

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:21.297
a new file is uploaded.

00:28:21.297 --> 00:28:22.630
Which does the same translation.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:27.320
So it's the same protocol
buffer-based format.

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:32.200
Now when we get
to the yellow box,

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:39.980
we have a pipeline for
processing the data,

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:43.220
structuring it so that it
doesn't use a lot of space,

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:47.720
and computing roll ups so that
you can do advanced queries

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:50.630
with millisecond latency.

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:53.780
And this pipeline, we've
run at 10 million data

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:54.640
points per second.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:58.670
So every component there, like
Kubernetes engine, AutoScaler,

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:04.130
Pub/Sub, scales incredibly
well, and Bigtable.

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.250
So it all scales horizontally.

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:07.670
And it's very fast.

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:11.210
And then when we do run
predictions on this data,

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:14.380
we use ML Engine to
host those models.

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:20.120
And we use Cloud Scheduler
to do periodic predictions.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:25.670
So we don't write back
to the control systems.

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:29.020
So the use cases that we
have are about advising,

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:30.118
say, the captain.

00:29:30.118 --> 00:29:31.660
You know, you need
to slow down a bit

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:35.110
because you're
using too much fuel.

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:37.880
Or you need to clean the hull,
because there's marine growth,

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.750
which is slowing you down.

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:43.570
It's something that you
can do periodically.

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:47.710
Maybe you do a
prediction every minute.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:52.560
But we do-- you
can think of cases

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:56.910
where you'd want to do
streaming, analytics,

00:29:56.910 --> 00:29:57.670
when it comes in.

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:02.610
But right now,
it's not necessary.

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:03.300
Security.

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:06.780
So the edge environment
is kind of scary.

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:09.120
The control systems
that you deal with there

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:13.760
are set up that they
usually don't have

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:17.010
the concept of authentication.

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:19.130
So if you can talk
to them, you can

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.880
do whatever you want with them.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.500
A couple of principles
that we imply,

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:26.060
we don't want to invent
their own encryption scheme.

00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:28.400
We let Google IoT
Core handle that.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:31.370
We have no inbound
routes to the edge.

00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:35.880
And there's a physically
separated network.

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:38.120
So we only read
to these sensors.

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:41.420
So that makes it a lot less
scary to be on the edge.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:45.790
But security is definitely
a big concern here.

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:47.860
So bringing that together.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:50.320
Here we have one
of the dashboards,

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:55.190
which will show you and verify
that the cargo was not damaged.

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:59.380
This is the ship's motion in
all the different directions,

00:30:59.380 --> 00:31:03.865
axles, at 10 Hertz with
200 milliseconds latency.

00:31:03.865 --> 00:31:05.740
So if you're shipping
cars, for instance, you

00:31:05.740 --> 00:31:07.510
could share this with
the car manufacturer

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:13.600
and really assert that you
didn't damage any of the goods.

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:16.370
It's also very important input
to the energy efficiency use

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:16.870
case.

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:19.450
Because if you have
rough seas, your engine

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:21.190
will have to work more.

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:25.900
In fact, very rough seas can
make the engines shut down

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:28.780
entirely, which is what
happened with the cruise ship,

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:31.060
Viking Sky, a
couple of weeks ago

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:32.940
outside the coast of Norway.

00:31:32.940 --> 00:31:35.860
We had rough seas
and the engines

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:38.740
tripped as a result of
that, leaving the ship

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:40.920
in a very precarious situation.

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:49.030
And then when we move on to the
more advanced use cases that

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:52.630
involve machine learning,
you have an, of course,

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:54.250
anomaly detection,
which is very much

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:56.050
used in predictive analytics.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:03.850
The models there
are usually based

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:06.220
on clustering or forecasting.

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:10.420
So you're looking for data
that you haven't seen before,

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:10.930
basically.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:15.230
And then you're
alerting someone.

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:17.400
Inspection.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.140
We have a lot of sensors
that are not just

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:23.750
pressure, temperature, flow.

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:27.680
We have a sensor in our
pocket, which is a camera.

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:30.800
And you can use that
to extract loads

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:34.890
of interesting information
using image recognition.

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:37.910
So inspection, and
we've trained--

00:32:37.910 --> 00:32:40.520
for instance, last year,
we trained an auto ML model

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:44.280
which detected damaged wires.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:48.300
And you can imagine having
drones to fly around and detect

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:52.950
these faulty things, or
corrosion, which is already

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:54.758
being done by some companies.

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:01.730
And in those cases,
you definitely

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:04.370
do not want to send all
the footage to the cloud

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:07.600
if you were on a
satellite connection.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:08.584
DANIELSON: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:16.900
Yeah.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:20.570
So, Geir, I heard some
rumors about your demo

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:22.460
last year, that you did some--

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:23.990
had the windmill
on stage and did

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.210
some machine learning as well?

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:28.010
That must have been
pretty terrifying.

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:28.910
GEIR ENGDAHL: Yes.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:33.470
A good demo is exciting to
the audience, and very scary

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:36.520
to the presenter.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:40.392
So I think, especially
when you have moving parts

00:33:40.392 --> 00:33:41.850
and you're doing
machine learning--

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:44.130
which is never really 100%--

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:46.080
that's always scary.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.410
But you know, the one trick is,
you can always blame the Wi-Fi.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:52.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:55.315
But yeah, so I
think we should we

00:33:55.315 --> 00:33:56.940
should try to top
that and do something

00:33:56.940 --> 00:33:58.380
even scarier this year.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.150
STEIN H. DANIELSON:
Yeah, definitely.

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:04.800
And that's why we brought this
tiny model of a chemical plant.

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:08.909
It continuously streams
live sensor data

00:34:08.909 --> 00:34:15.520
to Cognite Data Fusion
via Google IoT Core.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:19.230
So typically, you
use this sensor data

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:23.429
to visualize it
in charts like you

00:34:23.429 --> 00:34:27.100
can see soon on this screen.

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.010
So here you can see
the temperature,

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:31.440
it's about 27 degrees.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:32.790
That's pretty hot.

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:40.070
But there are other ways to
visualize your data, as well.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:48.679
In Cognite, we see the power
of using 3D models as a tool

00:34:48.679 --> 00:34:51.020
to visualize your data.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:55.260
And that is definitely, I
use a tool for the future.

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:56.489
GEIR ENGDAHL: I see that.

00:34:56.489 --> 00:34:58.935
But what if you don't
have a 3D model?

00:34:58.935 --> 00:35:01.760
STEIN H. DANIELSON:
That's definitely

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.650
a big issue and a
challenge for the industry.

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:07.770
Because either you
don't have a 3D model,

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:12.600
or perhaps the 3D
model already got

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:17.910
old from the day of assembly
and has become kind of useless.

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:21.420
So that's why we have
developed an application that

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:26.640
helps you generate these
up-to-date 3D models in order

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:30.775
to visualize your
data in a better way.

00:35:30.775 --> 00:35:33.322
GEIR ENGDAHL: You're
not connected, I think.

00:35:33.322 --> 00:35:36.155
STEIN H. DANIELSON: OK.

00:35:36.155 --> 00:35:38.275
GEIR ENGDAHL: It's the Wi-Fi.

00:35:38.275 --> 00:35:40.820
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah,
definitely the Wi-Fi.

00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:44.660
So what we can see here
is the model on screen.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:49.820
I take a single picture and
I detect features on it.

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:52.670
I could take one more,
and then it guides me

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:55.310
through the entire process.

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:59.780
So this is something that
I do now on my phone,

00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:04.200
but this is something that you
can do with any kind of camera.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:06.700
And it's not something that you
have to do yourself, either.

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:07.492
GEIR ENGDAHL: Yeah.

00:36:07.492 --> 00:36:09.460
So you just need the
photos for this to work,

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:10.960
you don't need a special camera.

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:13.970
You might wonder why
there's a helipad here.

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:19.120
That's because, sometimes when
we run this, we use a drone.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:21.333
Unfortunately, if
you look closely

00:36:21.333 --> 00:36:22.750
on the sign outside
the door here,

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:24.820
it says no drones are allowed.

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:30.430
And the complexity of getting
that permission in time

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:31.855
was unfortunately
too high for us.

00:36:31.855 --> 00:36:33.730
But it doesn't matter,
you can use anything--

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:41.233
like a mobile phone, or a drone,
or a GoPro on someone's helmet.

00:36:41.233 --> 00:36:42.275
It doesn't really matter.

00:36:42.275 --> 00:36:43.317
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah.

00:36:43.317 --> 00:36:45.580
GEIR ENGDAHL: So what's
happening to those pictures

00:36:45.580 --> 00:36:46.080
right now?

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:46.720
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:48.550
Now that I'm finished
taking the pictures,

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:51.790
I just upload them to
Cognite Data Fusion,

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:54.700
where the magic happens.

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:57.385
And right now, it's
processing all the pictures.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.790
And should we take a look
at the final results?

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:03.790
GEIR ENGDAHL: All right.

00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:04.456
Let's try that.

00:37:04.456 --> 00:37:05.081
Now I'm scared.

00:37:05.081 --> 00:37:07.590
STEIN H. DANIELSON: If
not, the Wi-Fi has failed.

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:15.920
GEIR ENGDAHL: Ooh, ooh!

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:16.420
Yes.

00:37:16.420 --> 00:37:18.520
And here it looks like
we got a nice model.

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:21.070
STEIN H. DANIELSON:
So suddenly we

00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:24.340
have a 3D model
that's up-to-date

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:28.150
with exact same dimension,
the exact same proportion.

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:31.523
And also, you get a good
overview of the texture

00:37:31.523 --> 00:37:32.440
on the model, as well.

00:37:35.555 --> 00:37:39.740
GEIR ENGDAHL: But if you
want to view the time

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:42.290
series in the right
context, here, I guess

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:43.790
you have to do some
kind of manual--

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:44.930
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah.

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:45.590
Yeah.

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:48.920
So the thing is, that this is
definitely a really good tool

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:50.950
for the future of the industry.

00:37:54.713 --> 00:37:56.130
But the thing is
that, wouldn't it

00:37:56.130 --> 00:38:00.000
be cool if we could link
this model with the data

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.800
that we have inside the
Cognite Data Fusion platform?

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:08.310
And that's actually
something that we can do.

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:11.850
So if we take a close
look on the tags that

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:18.480
are located all around this
model, you can see T-02, T-01.

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.280
Those kinds of tags
is something that

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:24.550
is present on all platforms,
or in ships, as well,

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:27.510
and identify the
different assets.

00:38:27.510 --> 00:38:29.730
That identifier is
something that we also

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:33.530
have in Cognite Data Fusion.

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:39.020
So what we can do, is to use the
same pictures that we took here

00:38:39.020 --> 00:38:44.960
and try to detect all
the different tags using

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:47.180
Google Vision.

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:49.400
And when we have
detected them, we

00:38:49.400 --> 00:38:53.330
store the location of
all the different assets,

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:56.496
and also which asset it is.

00:38:56.496 --> 00:38:59.060
GEIR ENGDAHL: So we'll see
if it got them in the right--

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:04.900
STEIN H. DANIELSON: And
the end result is here.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:07.380
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:09.733 --> 00:39:11.900
GEIR ENGDAHL: So what was
happening behind the scene

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:17.840
there, is we used a model called
YOLO, You Only Look Once, which

00:39:17.840 --> 00:39:20.030
is an object detection model.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:24.240
And that thing is trained
to look for these--

00:39:26.468 --> 00:39:27.760
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Tags there.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:28.718
GEIR ENGDAHL: The tags.

00:39:28.718 --> 00:39:30.560
But it can identify
other stuff too,

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:32.870
like rust, or faulty
wires, et cetera,

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:36.650
and place it right
there in the 3D model.

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:40.310
That's instant situational
awareness, there.

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:42.680
This room is too hot.

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:45.680
STEIN H. DANIELSON: So we
can demonstrate this for you.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:51.570
Let's see if I'm hotter or
cooler than 28.2 degrees.

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:52.200
So I'm cooler.

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:53.305
GEIR ENGDAHL: Yeah.

00:39:53.305 --> 00:39:54.960
STEIN H. DANIELSON:
I'm almost dead.

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:57.600
GEIR ENGDAHL: I guess maybe
you have some cold feet, too?

00:39:57.600 --> 00:39:58.800
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah.

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:01.853
But then we're inside the
operational limit again,

00:40:01.853 --> 00:40:02.520
and that's good.

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:04.610
Because it turns red
but it's above 27.

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:05.797
GEIR ENGDAHL: Yeah.

00:40:05.797 --> 00:40:07.380
You're actually
cooling the room down.

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:10.360
STEIN H. DANIELSON: Yeah.

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:12.200
So, yeah.

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:16.140
And this a model that
you can update 24/7,

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:19.660
at least when you have a robot
to take the pictures for you.

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:21.510
And that's really
convenient when

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:26.508
it comes to detect
when corrosion started.

00:40:26.508 --> 00:40:28.050
You can actually
travel back in time,

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.650
you can check whether the
corrosion mark was there

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:36.900
only two weeks ago, or
perhaps two years ago.

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:41.190
So to conclude, five
minutes ago, we only

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:45.690
had a chemical plant that
streamed live sensor data.

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:50.370
But now, we also have
an up-to-date 3D model

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:56.850
that displays our sensor values
in a really intuitive way.

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:58.180
Thank you.

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:00.555
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:03.405 --> 00:41:05.320
GEIR ENGDAHL: And
you can do this

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:08.110
with real industrial
equipment, too.

00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:11.810
This is data now streaming
in from the North Sea,

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:17.600
and it identified the plates
on that equipment, too.

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:19.342
And also, here, you
can find some things

00:41:19.342 --> 00:41:20.300
that shouldn't be here.

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:22.383
For instance, these ladders.

00:41:22.383 --> 00:41:24.050
They're not supposed
to be in this zone,

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:27.190
so it's a health safety
environment issue, right there.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:39.297
Then let's wrap up.

00:41:39.297 --> 00:41:40.130
Here's my checklist.

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:43.730
I wish this checklist was
shorter, but it's not.

00:41:43.730 --> 00:41:47.750
This is the reality of why
it's hard to create value

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:48.940
in industry.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:51.428
So you have to start with
the valuable use cases.

00:41:51.428 --> 00:41:53.720
Start with that, and talk to
the people in the business

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.070
because you will depend on
them to implement it later.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:42:00.070
So it better be something that
they think is valuable, too.

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:01.870
Then, you need to
figure out if you

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:05.470
have the data that's necessary
to solve those use cases.

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:07.960
Usually, you have
the sensors, but you

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:12.010
may have to do some work
to liberate the data.

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:14.860
And then you need
to clean the data,

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:18.160
make it so that you can
actually solve your use cases.

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:20.030
For instance, linking
it to a 3D model.

00:42:20.030 --> 00:42:22.120
It may seem trivial
when you have, kind of,

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:25.000
a plant with four sensors.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:27.850
But if you have 100,000 sensors,
and you send the data scientist

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:32.320
in to find the right sensors
to do predictive maintenance

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:34.450
on your one compressor
or whatever,

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:37.660
it's going to take months if you
don't have good tools to figure

00:42:37.660 --> 00:42:40.990
out what sensor is
where, and which,

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:44.101
and relevant to the use case.

00:42:44.101 --> 00:42:51.220
Then you need to do stuff with
the data, so that's algorithms.

00:42:51.220 --> 00:42:53.540
Usually, you don't
build your own.

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:58.450
You merely apply
other people's work.

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:01.280
Most companies don't do
machine learning research,

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:05.200
they do application of known
research to new problems.

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.880
Now once you're satisfied
with those results,

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:13.770
you need to monitor the
quality of the data.

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:19.050
Because your mobile probably
won't handle garbage data

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:19.590
coming in.

00:43:19.590 --> 00:43:21.060
And these systems,
these sensors,

00:43:21.060 --> 00:43:24.540
will give you a lot of crap
data from time to time.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:26.080
There will be
connectivity issues.

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:29.280
There will be flipping
bits here and there.

00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:35.730
It's a quite challenging
thing to do, so

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:38.980
have to build that
trust in the data.

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:41.310
And then you have to
change the way that people

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:42.697
work in the company.

00:43:42.697 --> 00:43:45.030
And there, it's very useful
if you've kind of introduced

00:43:45.030 --> 00:43:47.880
your use case early on,
so they know this is not

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:49.680
something you made up.

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:53.910
Worst case, you've
done a lot of work,

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:55.410
and you get to this
stage, and it's

00:43:55.410 --> 00:43:58.890
like, hey, this isn't useful.

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:01.350
That sucks.

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:06.170
And then last, that's when
you've done all those things.

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:06.750
You profit.

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:09.110
And that's what IoT
is really about,

00:44:09.110 --> 00:44:10.550
otherwise it's not sustainable.

00:44:13.740 --> 00:44:16.530
Here are some pointers
to get you started.

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:20.310
So for IoT Core, all the
AI solutions that Google

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:26.070
has to offer, we have a
booth down in the lower level

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:27.770
and there are
industry solutions.

00:44:27.770 --> 00:44:32.140
So it's on the left after
taking the escalator down.

00:44:32.140 --> 00:44:35.540
Come talk to us there
if you have questions.

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:36.040
Thank you.

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:41.380
And also, don't forget
to rate this talk.

00:44:41.380 --> 00:44:45.040
We definitely appreciate your
feedback so that, you know,

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:47.770
next year we can have an
even better talk and even

00:44:47.770 --> 00:44:50.600
scarier demo.

00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:51.310
Thank you.

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:52.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:55.260
